  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable weather continues for tonight and\n Wednesday. \n\nDry subsident W to NW flow continues into Wednesday as southern New \nEngland remains under a flattened mid-level ridge. A moisture-\nstarved shortwave drops southward overnight. This will mainly bring \na period of breezy conditions overnight, mainly along the coast and \ninterior higher elevations overnight. This will also bring in a \nslightly cooler airmass aloft by Wednesday. At most, temperatures \nmay be a degree or two cooler than Tuesday. Highs will likely rise \ninto the low to mid 50s with the exception of the CT Valley with \nupper 50s in spots. Mid/upper level moisture will support some cloud \ncover mixed in with sunshine. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chilly rain likely late Thu into Sat, with some \nsnow possible in the higher elevations of MA/CT\n\nGood model (AI and traditional models) agreement on a coastal low or \nseries of lows tracking somewhere along or south of New England in \nthe Thu-Sat timeframe. Although, there is considerable uncertainty \nin the guidance on multi waves vs one consolidate low, along with \nthe magnitude/intensity of each wave. Therefore, large differences \non QPF, axis of heaviest QPF, along with location/track of dry slot. \nAs for ptype, given the lack of cold air (warm blyr), most of the \nregion will likely experience a chilly rain, especially the coastal \nplain and big population centers of Boston, Worcester, Providence \nand Hartford. For snow in the coastal plain to occur with this \nsetup, a stronger system would be required, with strong height falls \ncooling the column from top down to erode warm boundary layer. Also, \nheavy precip would be required to cool the column via diabatic \ncooling from melting, and models show a very large spread in axis of \nheaviest QPF. Thus, a low confidence forecast on exact storm \ndetails. As for timing of precip, most 12z guidance, including the \n12z REFS, Thu may begin dry, but by days end, most guidance has rain \nfalling over all of MA/CT/RI. \n\nA look at the ensembles, both AI and traditional models, only one or \ntwo members from both the EC and GFS ensembles have substantial \nsnowfall across MA/CT/RI. Thus, non-zero, but very low probability \nof this happening. Again, it would require a stronger system with \nsignificant height falls and heavy precip over SNE. Hence, a chilly \nrain for most of MA/RI/CT, with some snow possible across the higher \nelevations of MA/CT.   \n\nAs for snow probs from the ensembles, the 12z GEFS ensembles only \nhave 10-20% probability for 3+ inches across Northern Worcester and \nthe Berkshires. The CMC ensembles are very similar and the 12z ECENS \nhave up to 50-60% of 3+ inches across the Berks, with 30-40% over \nNorthern Worcester county. \n\nEnsembles suggest low slowly exits by Sunday, with some height rises \nover SNE. Thus, some improvement Sunday with drier weather and not \nas chilly as Sat, although remaining blustery Sunday. Additional \nimprovement comes early next week with further height rises \npromoting seasonable temps (highs low 50s) and dry weather.",
